WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
Good Morning, Hank, its Tuesday. So, remember Brotherhood 2.0 resident mathematician Daniel Biss?

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:07.000
He was a friend of mine, and a very successful math professor at the University of Chicago

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:13.920
before deciding to leave that job and run for political office whereupon he eventually became a very successful state senator.

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:17.160
Yeah, so Daniel is now running for Governor of Illinois,

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:19.820
like, complete with fancy campaign ads and everything.

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:24.160
And I have to say, this turn of events is a little surprising to me, because Daniel is the smartest person I know

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:26.480
and Governor of Illinois seems like a

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:27.760
a terrible job.

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:32.220
For one thing, Illinois politics are so dysfunctional that the state regularly has no budget.

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:36.240
And, for another, four of Illinois's past ten governors have been imprisoned for corruption,

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:40.980
an incarceration rate that is, you know, somewhat higher than the one seen among professors of mathematics.

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:48.300
Anyway, what interests me most is that Daniel, deep down, wants to work in government to help people, and because he believes in democracy.

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:50.020
And he's actually been very successful as a state senator.

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:56.020
He led the passage of legislation to increase retirement savings, and a ban on so-called "conversion therapy" for LGBT people.

00:00:56.020 --> 00:01:00.740
But, to accomplish stuff, he's had to wade through this vast, gridlocked swamp of US politics,

00:01:00.740 --> 00:01:04.200
and also, he's had to attend many, many meetings.

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:08.680
Hank, sometimes when we talk about politics, or the news, people in comments will be like, "Why don't you run for office?"

00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:13.460
And the short answer is that being a politician means going to meetings all the time, and I hate meetings with a white-hot passion.

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:17.140
Like, when I'm forced to attend a meeting, by far my most common contribution is to say,

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:19.220
"So, are we done, then?"

00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:24.680
I usually start saying that like 12 seconds into a meeting, and then repeat it, mantra-like, until finally I am released.

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:25.420
Slight tangent:

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:30.580
The other day, I was playing Ping-Pong with Stan, and I was telling him how happy I am about the Crash Courses in Computer Science and World Mythology,

00:01:30.580 --> 00:01:33.580
and he was like, "Yeah, do you want to do some more Crash Course: Literature in the fall?"

00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:34.320
And I was like, "Maybe."

00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:36.640
And he was like, "We could read 1984, some other-"

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:41.500
And I was like, "Wait a second, Stan, did you just use the magic of Ping-Pong to trick me into a damn meeting?"

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:43.580
So, anyway, more Crash Course: Literature coming this fall.

00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:47.460
But, to return to the point, politicians have to attend so many meetings.

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:50.160
Like, say you're running for governor here in my home state of Indiana.

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:52.900
You have to have tons of meetings with rich people to ask them for money,

00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:58.060
and then every day you have to have meetings with strangers all around the state to hear their concerns.

00:01:58.060 --> 00:02:01.480
Many of these people will disagree with your political positions, and will never vote for you,

00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:05.600
and yet you have to listen to them like a grown-up and resist the urge to cover your ears and say,

00:02:05.660 --> 00:02:07.540
Na, Na, Na, Na, Na, Na, Na, Na, Na, Na, Na, Na, Na, Na, Na, Na

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:14.540
And then, you have to shake hands with these strangers, even though human hands are notoriously filthy and handshakes always remind you of that story about someone coming up to James Joyce

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:19.420
and saying, "Can I kiss the hand that wrote Ulysses?", and Joyce saying, "No, because it's done so many other things as well."

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:22.220
And then, if you're good enough at shaking hands and meeting with rich people

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:25.140
and meeting at town halls and meeting with other elected officials

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:27.100
et cetera, literally ad nauseam,

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:28.240
You get to be Governor.

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:31.220
At which point you basically become a professional attender of meetings.

00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:33.960
Hank, do you know what the Governor of Indiana is doing right now?

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:37.720
He is attending a public meeting of the Indiana Economic Development Corporation.

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:40.860
I mean if there is a hell and I end up there I will spend eternity thinking:

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:44.640
"Well, at least I am not at a meeting of the Indiana Economic Development Corporation.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:48.740
So yeah, my hatred of meetings, among other things, would make me a terrible elected official

00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:50.660
but that doesn't mean elected officials are terrible.

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:53.960
In fact, the only one I know is among the kindest,

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:55.960
most thoughtful people I've ever met

00:02:55.960 --> 00:02:57.960
and no matter your political leanings, you'd be lucky

00:02:57.960 --> 00:02:59.360
to have him as your governor.

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:02.660
Now, of course, too many politicians do embody our caricatures of them

00:03:02.660 --> 00:03:05.360
but our system of government depends upon Daniel

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:07.720
and the many, many people like him.

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:10.220
People who are passionate and honest public servants

00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:15.080
and who are willing to attend many, many meetings so that I don't have to

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:16.820
We desperately need those people in government,

00:03:16.820 --> 00:03:20.480
so Daniel, thank you, good luck, I know you're running against a few billionaires

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:21.400
but I believe in you.

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:23.400
Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:26.120
End... Screen. Hey, so you know Self-Care Bunny?

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:28.120
Rosianna's "Self Care Bunny" is now available in beautiful poster form

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:30.120
at DFTBA.com.

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:32.120
Also a lot of you have been asking about the boxes in the background--

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:33.700
I don't know what's in them.

00:03:33.700 --> 00:03:35.820
but in a completely unrelated story, as you might know,

00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:37.460
there is a scavenger hunt happening.

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:39.480
There's a different riddle every week, it's a little bit complicated...

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:40.940
I'll explain more in the doobly-doo.

00:03:40.940 --> 00:03:44.360
But for those of you who like riddles, this week's clue actually IS Self-Care Bunny

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:44.980
Well

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:48.600
Rearranged......

